Thank You Ian and what a privilege it is  to be with this congregate  this sacred sacred day in the Christian  year when I was a little girl the  biggest thing that happened every year  in my hometown of Wilmington North  Carolina was the Azalea festival will  meet n is a town on the southeastern  coast of North Carolina that is  ordinarily known for local by locals for  two things the battleship North Carolina  that is docked there and flowers Guns  and Roses perhaps well in this case guns  and azaleas I grew up taking the beauty  of the city for granted I didn't realize  how wonderful it was to live in a city  where something bloomed all year long in  the winter it was the camellias deep  pink and white and variegated varieties  that my father loved to cut cut and  float in vases all through our house or  to send home without a town guests  nestled in a bed of Spanish moss and in  the spring of the year the whole city  came ablaze with dogwoods and azaleas so  to celebrate its beauty every year  Wilmington still has an azalea festival  which for children at least culminated  on Saturday morning with the Azalea  festival parade I still remember going  to my first parade when I was nine years  old and we had just moved to Wilmington  it was complete with marching bands and  baton twirlers and floats with  beautifully decked out people on them  and the parade would weave its way  through downtown Wilmington while crowds  lined the streets someone had given me  an autograph book as a gift that year  this was a little blank book in which i  think i was supposed to collect all the  signatures of all the famous people i  would meet in my life so i stood at that  parade side and every time a float would  stop in front of our family i would run  up lift my autograph book up high and  have someone on that float sign it the  prize autograph though was that of the  Azalea queen every year some second-tier  TV or  star or movie star came to our home town  and was wined and dined for a weekend  and got to be queen of our parade when I  was nine years old the Queen was Shelley  Fabares now I don't expect many of you  here have a clue who she was she starred  in the Donna Reed show which was a  popular show in the 50s of my childhood  oh do I still remember reaching my  autograph book up and getting Shelley  Fabares to sign it somehow when I  picture the first Palm Sunday parade I  picture it being sort of like my  hometown I say a festival parade Jesus  the celebrity is coming to town and it's  a big deal for all the crowds that have  gathered in the city for the annual  feast of the Passover throngs of adults  and children lined the streets that go  through the Kidron Valley up the winding  road to the walled city of Jerusalem  there on the hill the temple gleams in  the sunlight and the excitement is  palpable as street vendors sell their  wares and children ask their parents to  put them on their shoulders so they can  get a better glimpse now four hours are  in short supply in this arid land so  people cut branches off of palm trees or  bring sheaves of wheat and wave them  wildly as they wait for Jesus to arrive  and when he finally gets there the  crowds cheer Imam with gusto Hosanna  Hosanna blessed is the one who comes in  the name of the Lord they sing psalms  like they sang for the Great King David  when he would ride that path up to  Jerusalem and they hailed Jesus as the  long awaited King of the Jews yet the  funny thing is if you look closely  things are not as they should be in this  parade in fact it's pretty much a parody  of what it should be or narrowly a king  would ride into town on a big stallion  showing his power and his might and  Jesus chooses to ride in on a little  Colt that has not even been broken for  riding or narrowly a king  right into Jerusalem and full military  regalia flanked by weapons of war  showing forth the military strength  showing the will to overthrow the Romans  but Jesus comes in a non-violent and  peaceable way ordinary a king would ride  into the city with politics foremost on  his mind graciously thanking the crowds  for their welcome commenting pleasantly  on the beauty of their city in its  temple promising to up build both during  their brain and what does Jesus do one  of his very first acts after getting to  town is to go to the temple in a holy  rage overturn the money changers there  who were buying and selling sacrificial  animals and declare is it not written  that my house shall be a house of prayer  for all nations and you have turned it  into a den of robbers woof when I tried  to imagine this parade in a contemporary  scene I picture King Jesus riding into  my hometown of Wilmington not on a float  of flowers but maybe on a moped he might  even be wearing a clown suit with a  painted tier on one cheek when the  parade ends the crowd waits expectantly  for Jesus to go to the podium said it's  up at the end of the parade where all  the dignitaries are to thank them for  hosting him and instead he strides down  market street and into one of the old  historic churches there and starts  turning over the tables that advertise  the church night suppers and the  basketball tournaments he reminds the  political leaders of my hometown that  though the city is beautiful on the  outside the beauty hides some  deep-seated ugliness of racial  discrimination and Prejudice he reminds  them that in 1898 when the white  Aristarchus aristocracy of Wilmington  were upstaged in an election they  conspire  to run the duly elected black majority  out of town and the Cape Fear River  literally ran red with the blood of the  black leaders massacred he reminds the  church leaders and the good church folk  that they were complicit errs in this  discrimination and violence and that  they have refused to work for the things  that make for peace now don't get me  wrong I don't mean to pick on my home  town today I simply know its history  better than I know that of other cities  my guess is if Jesus came to New Haven  he would find equally offensive deeds  and acts to be angry about after all is  it this the place where the Divinity  School where I teach was built on land  purchased with remington gun money the  city where this very university sits on  land once formed by the Quinnipiac  Indians the town where in 1831 the  citizens voted by a majority of 700 24  to reject a proposal that the first  african-american college in the nation  be established here now I don't single  out Wilmington because it's more to  blame I only make the analogy so we can  imagine how furious the religious  leaders must have been with Jesus and  why Jesus does not play the role of  gracious accepting guests Jesus does not  play the role of hoped for King Jesus  does not play the role of honored  religious dignitary preaching a good  sermon and blessing everybody for coming  out to hear him instead through this  parody of a parade Jesus up ends all  illusions that the good folk have that  they are indeed good he confronts them  with their failure to live up to the  truths of God to God's will for  righteousness and justice and equality  and  life is it any wonder they despised him  is it any wonder that by weeks in the  crowd had changed from Hosanna to  crucify him get him out of this town two  colleagues of mine in the field of  preaching chuck campbell and johann  seniors have recently written a book  with the provocative title that I also  chose for my sermon today Jesus The Fool  in it they maintained that what was  going on that first palm sunday was  Street Theatre in which Jesus through  his intentional parody of the powers and  principalities of this world was  speaking out against the ways that were  against God Jesus they say is no passive  victim in Mark's Gospel silently going  toward the cross Jesus is acting out  Jesus is acting out in nonviolent  protest jesus literally plays the fool  in order to disrupt the current social  order and to convince folk that there is  a more profound truth the truth of God  if this is so then the question for us  becomes do we dare join Jesus in his  foolish parade do we dare join him in  his nonviolent resistance of the  principalities and powers of our day  well we dare become ourselves as the  Apostle Paul put it fools for Christ's  sake I would suggest to you that if we  looked out the windows over here today  out on chapel street and watch the  parade as Jesus leads it through town we  would see some other followers who have  chosen to be fools in that parade I  think I see Archbishop Desmond Tutu up  front there  you know the one who persistently  non-violently opposed with his whole  being apartheid in South Africa and who  one day in a pretty lampooning kind of  manner announced to the prime minister  of south africa and the heyday apartheid  mr. prime minister we must remind you  that you are just a man you are not God  and one day your name shall merely be a  faint scribble on the pages of history  while the name of jesus christ the lord  of the church will live forever and back  there I think I see William Sloane  coffin who used to preach right here in  this Chapel foolishly burning the draft  cards of vietnam war resisters and with  his ever humorous turn a phrase saying  to us you know we are beginning to  resemble extinct dinosaurs who suffered  from too much armor and too little brain  and look over there I think I see some  members of middle Collegiate Church in  New York City who last week all wear  more hoodies to church as a part of  their nonviolent protest against the  killing of Trayvon Martin and so many  youth like him and there you just might  see some folk from occupy wall street  movement right across the street joining  in line foolishly trying to overturn the  money tables with Jesus and saying  corruption and government has got to go  there are people in our land who are  suffering suffering because of the  inequality of wealth you see the truth  of the matter is I don't think Jesus  wants to write his name in our autograph  books today I think Jesus wants to write  his name on our hearts and in our lives  I think Jesus wants to recruit some more  people who were willing to dare to be  fools for his sake  so today he invites us first to come to  this table where the ultimate  foolishness of God in Christ is revealed  to us as our King is sacrificed on a  cross out of love for us to take his  body and blood into our bodies and our  blood and then to go out in this world  to be fools for him the parade is  passing by do we dare join its ranks
 

